---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import {appUrl} from '../lib/appUrl';

const app = appUrl(import.meta.env.MODE);
---

<Base
  title="About · Command Snippets"
  description="Why Command Snippets exists and how it works: tagged command snippets, ordered your way, one click from your clipboard, private by default and yours to back up."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Command Snippets is a note-taking system for technical professionals:
      programmers, system administrators, and anyone whose work runs on
      commands they’d rather not work out twice.
    </p>

    <p>
      Command Snippets is operated by Tearleads, LLC. For product help,
      privacy requests or questions about the service, <a href="/contact/">contact us</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      The hard part of a command is rarely typing it. It’s finding the flag,
      the query or the incantation the first time, then finding it again six
      months later. Shell history forgets, and general-purpose notes bury the
      one line you need under everything else.
    </p>

    <h2>An opinionated shape</h2>
    <p>
      In Command Snippets a note is a snippet: a subject to find it by, and a
      body you’ll paste. Snippets live in tags, a snippet can sit in as many
      tags as it belongs to, and you put every tag in the order you think in.
      A click copies a snippet, and every copy is counted, so the snippets you
      rely on show it. That’s most of it, on purpose.
    </p>

    <h2>What it promises</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Private by default.</strong> Your tags and snippets are yours
        alone until you choose to make some public, and how often you reuse a
        snippet never appears on a public page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local first.</strong> Every change saves on your device at once
        and reaches the server when it can, so the app keeps working offline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Yours to keep.</strong> Export a backup of everything whenever
        you like, and restore it later; what a restore replaces is kept as an
        earlier version.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="prose-cta">
      <a class="login primary" href={app}>Get started</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
